Looking at this problem, I need to determine whether an isomorphism $f: A \times B \to A \times C$ commuting with projections to $A$ forces $B \cong C$.

**Answer: No, this does not imply $B \cong C$ in general.**

## Counterexample

Consider the poset category $\mathcal{C}$ corresponding to the totally ordered set $\{0 < a < 1\}$. This category has all binary products: the product of two objects is their meet (minimum), which exists for every pair since the poset is a finite total order. The terminal object is $1$.

Set $A = a$, $B = 1$, $C = a$.

**Computing the products:**
- $A \times B = a \wedge 1 = a$. The projection $p: a \to A = a$ is the unique morphism $a \to a$, i.e., $\text{id}_a$.
- $A \times C = a \wedge a = a$. The projection $q: a \to A = a$ is likewise $\text{id}_a$.

**The isomorphism:** Take $f = \text{id}_a : a \to a$. This is an isomorphism, and:
$$q \circ f = \text{id}_a \circ \text{id}_a = \text{id}_a = p. \quad\checkmark$$

**But $B \not\cong C$:** We have $B = 1$ and $C = a$. There is a morphism $a \to 1$ (since $a < 1$), but there is no morphism $1 \to a$ (since $1 \not\leq a$). Hence no isomorphism $B \to C$ exists.

This gives a valid counterexample where $A$ is even non-initial (the initial object is $0 \neq a$).

## Remark: when the implication *does* hold

The implication $B \cong C$ becomes true under the additional hypothesis that $A$ admits a **global element**, i.e., a morphism $\alpha: 1 \to A$ from the terminal object. The argument is a fiber/pullback construction:

The fiber of $p: A\times B \to A$ over $\alpha$ is the pullback $1 \times_A (A \times B)$. By the universal property of the product, this pullback is canonically isomorphic to $B$: the map $B \to A \times B$ given by $\langle \alpha \circ !_B,\, \text{id}_B\rangle$ exhibits $B$ as this pullback (with $!_B: B \to 1$ the unique map). Similarly, $1 \times_A (A \times C) \cong C$.

Since $f$ satisfies $q \circ f = p$, it is a morphism over $A$, and pulling back along $\alpha$ yields an isomorphism:
$$1 \times_A f \;:\; \underbrace{1 \times_A (A \times B)}_{\cong\, B} \;\xrightarrow{\;\sim\;}\; \underbrace{1 \times_A (A \times C)}_{\cong\, C}$$

(since pullbacks preserve isomorphisms). Composing with the canonical identifications gives $B \cong C$.

In the counterexample above, $A = a$ has no global element: a morphism $1 \to a$ in the poset would require $1 \leq a$, which is false. This is precisely why the fiber argument cannot be applied and the implication fails.

